      "text": "The sum of the angles in a pentagon is $540^\\circ$.\n\nWe are given that angles $BAD$ and $BCD$ are $128^\\circ$ and $111^\\circ$, respectively.\n\nThe sum of the angles within $\\triangle ABC$ is $180^\\circ$.\n\nWe start by noting the value of angle $ABC$:\n\n\\[\n\\angle ABC = 540^\\circ - 128^\\circ - 111^\\circ - 113^\\circ\n\\]\n\nThus,\n\n\\[\n\\angle ABC = 540^\\circ - 352^\\circ = 188^\\circ\n\\]\n\nIn $\\triangle ABC$, the third angle of $\\angle ACB$ can be found as follows:\n\n\\[\n\\angle ACB = \\angle ABC - \\angle BAC\n\\]\n\nWe already know $\\angle BAC = 188^\\circ - 111^\\circ - 113^\\circ = 2^\\circ$. Therefore,\n\n\\[\n\\angle ACB = 188^\\circ - 2^\\circ = 186^\\circ\n\\]\n\nNow we need to find angle $P$, which is the external angle opposite to $\\angle ACB$ in the revised pentagon. The sum of the angles at point $P$ is $360^\\circ$:\n\n\\[\n\\angle APD + \\angle BPC + \\angle CPD + \\angle DPE + \\angle P = 360^\\circ\n\\]\n\nSince $\\angle CPD = 186^\\circ$ and $\\angle DPE = 111^\\circ$, we substitute these values into our equation:\n\n\\[\n\\angle APD + \\angle BPC + 186^\\circ + 111^\\circ + \\angle P = 360^\\circ\n\\]\n\nSubstituting the given angles, we have:\n\n\\[\n352^\\circ + \\angle APD + \\angle BPC + \\angle P = 360^\\circ\n\\]\n\nSimplifying, we get:\n\n\\[\n\\angle APD + \\angle BPC + \\angle P = 8^\\circ\n\\]\n\nSince we need the measure of angle $P$, we focus on $\\angle APD = \\angle BPC$ and know that $\\angle APD + \\angle BPC = 8^\\circ$:\n\n\\[\n2 \\angle APD = 8^\\circ\n\\]\n\nThus,\n\n\\[\n\\angle APD = \\angle BPC = 4^\\circ\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the measure of angle $P$ is $4^\\circ$. The final answer is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{4}\n\\]",
