      "text": "To find the measure of angle \\( P \\), we need to use the fact that the sum of the interior angles of a pentagon (a shape with five sides) is given by the formula:\n\n\\[\n\\text{Sum of interior angles} = (n-2) \\times 180^\\circ\n\\]\n\nwhere \\( n \\) is the number of sides of the polygon. For a pentagon, \\( n = 5 \\), so the sum of the interior angles is:\n\n\\[\n(5-2) \\times 180^\\circ = 3 \\times 180^\\circ = 540^\\circ\n\\]\n\nWe are given the measures of four of the angles: \\( 128^\\circ \\), \\( 92^\\circ \\), \\( 113^\\circ \\), and \\( 111^\\circ \\). Let's denote the measure of angle \\( P \\) as \\( x \\). The sum of these five angles is:\n\n\\[\n128^\\circ + 92^\\circ + 113^\\circ + 111^\\circ + x = 540^\\circ\n\\]\n\nNow, we can substitute the known values into the equation:\n\n\\[\n128^\\circ + 92^\\circ + 113^\\circ + 111^\\circ + x = 540^\\circ\n\\]\n\nAdding the known angles together, we get:\n\n\\[\n444^\\circ + x = 540^\\circ\n\\]\n\nTo find \\( x \\), we subtract \\( 444^\\circ \\) from both sides of the equation:\n\n\\[\nx = 540^\\circ - 444^\\circ\n\\]\n\n\\[\nx = 96^\\circ\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the measure of angle \\( P \\) is \\(\\boxed{96}\\).",
